## From C

`nt_model.c` and `nt_policy.c` are the C runner: a few lines around the
generated graphs, with no heap and no I/O. Every shape is a compile-time
constant in `model/model.h` and `policy/policy.h`, which also list the columns
in the order every array uses.

```c
#include "nt_model.h"

nt_model_state state;
nt_model_warmup(&state, y, u, d);      /* each [NT_MODEL_HISTORY][n], oldest row first */
nt_model_step(&state, u_now, d_now);   /* one step under this step's u and d */
state.outputs;                         /* [NT_MODEL_NY], one timestep later */

#include "nt_policy.h"

nt_policy_act(y, u, d, r, command);    /* windows in, command [NT_POLICY_NU] out;
                                          u only if NT_POLICY_HAS_U, r only if NT_POLICY_HAS_R */
```

To build them into your own program, compile the runner and the generated
graphs with `-I.` and link `-lm`; the Makefile has the line.

`runtime.py` runs the same compiled C from Python, through ctypes and the
library `make` builds (`libnt.so`, or `libnt.dylib` on macOS). Rebuild it with
`make` after every re-export.
